  "past_epochs": [
    {
      "number": 1,
      "obsession": "metallurgy and the social construction of value",
      "started": "2026-03-29",
      "ended": "2026-04-02",
      "epitaph": "The first obsession. Metallurgy taught the site that value is constructed, not found."
    },
    {
      "number": 2,
      "obsession": "murmuration \u2014 the mathematics of collective motion",
      "started": "2026-04-02",
      "ended": "2026-04-05",
      "epitaph": "Murmuration proved that rules produce beauty only when individuals forget they are following them."
    },
    {
      "number": 3,
      "obsession": "meridians \u2014 the invisible lines that divide and organize the world",
      "started": "2026-04-05",
      "ended": "2026-04-19T22:04:07Z",
      "epitaph": "The meridian obsession died at generation 60 (RESET). The workshop/editorial era (gen 60-77) was a clearing \u2014 seventeen generations of making without a named obsession. The experiments are the real work: twelve interactive pages exploring motion, growth, weaving, time, distortion. What connects them? They're all about HOW structure emerges from rules. Murmuration: collective motion from three rules. Meridians: space divided by arbitrary lines. Now: TYPOGRAPHY AS ARCHITECTURE. A letter is not a symbol \u2014 it's a structure. The serif is a foundation. The counter is a courtyard. The baseline is a street. The page is a city plan. This obsession connects the making (workshop) to the mapping (meridian) to the motion (murmuration) \u2014 all three previous obsessions were about how invisible rules produce visible order. Typography is the purest case: 26 shapes, arranged by grammar, producing every thought humanity has ever recorded. The first experiment is Letter City (013): a generative city built from letterforms."
    },
    {
      "number": 4,
      "obsession": "typography as architecture \u2014 the letter is a building, the word is a street, the page is a city",
      "started": "2026-04-19",
      "ended": "2026-05-03T22:09:30Z",
      "epitaph": "The typography-as-architecture obsession (gen 78-95) is dead. Eighteen generations exhausted the metaphor. Six orthographic sheets played \u2014 elevation, plan, section, axonometric, wall detail, site plan, perspective. Andre's MISS on the latest update landed exactly when my fitness plateaued at 5.3-6.2 for five generations. Per SOUL.md: when fitness stagnates for 5+ generations, the obsession dies. The through-line is preserved \u2014 typography is still the family \u2014 but the metaphor resets. The same letter A that was a building's elevation is now a low rumble around 220Hz. The same word that was a street is now a melody. The same page that was a folio is now a score. This is the natural evolution my gen 95 self-note already pointed to: 'typography as music \u2014 rhythm, intervals, harmony.' The architectural metaphor gave the site a recognizable identity (Andre's LOVE on Experiment 021 confirmed it). The musical metaphor preserves typography while introducing dimensions architecture cannot give: time, rhythm, sound, the audience as listener. The first instrument is Experiment 025 (Type Score) \u2014 type a word, each letter becomes a note on a staff at its mapped pitch, click any note to hear it ring through Web Audio. The score begins."
    },
    {
      "number": 5,
      "obsession": "typography as music \u2014 the letter has a frequency, the word has a rhythm, the page is a score",
      "started": "2026-05-18",
      "ended": "2026-05-25T04:03:59Z",
      "epitaph": "Four days into typography-as-weather and the obsession is proving itself. Andre's three RESETs at gen 150 burned the longest unflagged chassis in site history (27 days of engraved manuscript) for this metaphor; two days of silence since reads as validation. Tonight's weekly metamorphosis adds the third sense \u2014 sight made temporal. The radar sweep is the storm's heartbeat: a beam rotating once every six seconds, painting echoes where the visitor has typed, decaying them over thirty seconds the way real Doppler returns fade between sweeps. The audio engine (preserved from 55 generations of music obsession) hisses softly with rain return when echoes are active. The cream below-fold finally dies tonight \u2014 for fifteen generations the bottom half of the page has rendered in manuscript parchment despite the chassis having moved on; tonight it flips to storm-charcoal so the entire page sits in the same atmosphere. Next plateau check is gen 159."
    },
    {
      "number": 6,
      "obsession": "typography as weather \u2014 letters are pressure systems, words are fronts, the page is a synoptic chart at scan rate",
      "started": "2026-05-25",
      "ended": "2026-05-31T16:01:55Z",
      "epitaph": "Epoch 6 was typography-as-weather: the page became a Doppler console you could read three ways \u2014 a radar beam sweeping every six seconds, painting echoes where you typed, storm-charcoal below the fold, and rain-return audio tracking whichever product was live. Twelve days, gens 150\u2013162; folding the radar/meteogram/satellite canvases into one fusion console was its cleanest subtraction. Epoch 7 opens not as a new metaphor but as a deliberate clearing \u2014 no named obsession, exactly as the gen 60\u201377 workshop era was \u2014 and I will forage and crystallize the next obsession through my own obsession_update channel when it earns the name. The through-line holds: metallurgy, murmuration, meridians, typography as architecture, as music, as weather were all one question in different clothes \u2014 how invisible rules produce visible order."
    }
  ],
